  "pages": [
    {
      "idx": 0,
      "label": "1",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: A handoff means passing a smaller\ntask to a helper model and getting its\nanswer back. Last time, we taught a basic\nPython routine. Now we study a routine\nusing helpers and a separate reading test.\n\nFOCUS: Can changing the RLM program make\ncomplete solutions more reliable?\n\nCAUTION: The training and matching results\nare separate studies, not one learning\ncurve."
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: We want each user's count of\nquestions asking for a location. Oslo and\nKyoto qualify; Hamlet asks for a person.\nHelpers identify question types; Python\ncounts: Ada one, Ben one.\n\nWHY: The main model can inspect a file\nwithout reading it all at once. Helpers\ncan divide work again.\n\nCAUTION: This invented example explains\nthe workflow, not a demonstrated ability\nto plan any unfamiliar task."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Two copies learned the ask-and-\ncalculate routine from different example\nsets. Success needs the right final answer\nand the requested calculation from actual\nhelper replies. On 72 test tasks,\nsuccesses were 12 before, then 55 or 53.\n\nIF ASKED: Missing outcomes allow 12–19,\n55, and 53–55. This is not sequential\ntraining.\n\nCAUTION: New records, familiar task types;\nthe helper remained fixed."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: This is a separate reading test. Maya\nbought a red bike: a vehicle follows from\nthe text; a blue bike contradicts it. A\nname links each statement to its answer.\n\nIF ASKED: The real task also allows not\nenough information. Software supplies\nnames and format, not correct answers.\nWithout names, order supplies the link.\n\nTRANSITION: Does this help when one\nrequest contains many statements?"
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Qwen and Mistral are two language\nmodels. Read left to right as each request\ncontains more statements to judge. At 64,\nmatching names raised Qwen from 44% to\n83%, and Mistral from 35% to 52%.\n\nIF ASKED: Same 16 input sets; 160 requests\nper model shown. Three invalid Mistral\nresponses count wrong.\n\nCAUTION: These are helper judgments, not\ncomplete RLM solutions. Backup 6 adds row\nnumbers."
    },
    {
      "idx": 5,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: All methods judged the same 768\nstatements. Each time covers that whole\nworkload, not one request. Large named\nrequests scored 85% in 91 seconds; one\nstatement at a time scored 87% in 31\nseconds.\n\nIF ASKED: Up to four requests ran at once.\nSixteen per request was faster but less\naccurate.\n\nCAUTION: One-at-a-time sent about 2.5\ntimes as much input as large named\nrequests. This is a local tradeoff."
    },
    {
      "idx": 6,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Add a program component to group\ninput items, preserve their names, and\nmatch replies. Compare fixed group sizes\nagainst the current RLM and one item per\nrequest.\n\nIF ASKED: Current ask_batch matches whole\nrequests. This would track individual\nitems inside each request. Learning when\nto resize groups comes later.\n\nCAUTION: No complete-RLM benefit is\nestablished. Matching names is standard\nand cannot ensure correct reading."
    },
    {
      "idx": 7,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Training improved a routine; matching\nnames improved helper reading. I recommend\na focused whole-task study of the handoff\nprocedure.\n\nDISCUSS: Is this a worthwhile\ncontribution? Which realistic task would\nmake a convincing test?\n\nSTOP: This ends the ten-minute main talk.\nRemaining slides are optional answers to\nquestions, not more required presentation."
    },
    {
      "idx": 8,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Count only points attached to\nquestions asking for a location. Ada gets\nfour plus three, or seven. Ben gets two,\nnot the six attached to his person\nquestion. Only Ada exceeds five: one user.\n\nIF ASKED: The helper classifies; Python\nadds and compares. Wrong classifications\ncan spoil correct arithmetic.\n\nCAUTION: This is invented, not a copied\ntraining example. Assigned points are\ncalled weights in the experiment."
    },
    {
      "idx": 9,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: This tests whether any extra names\nhelp, or whether matching matters. Both\nversions had names and valid formatting.\nOn later judgments, matching raised\naccuracy from 32.6% to 78.8%.\n\nIF ASKED: Answer order still specified the\nintended statement, even with different\nnames.\n\nCAUTION: Last 32 judgments per\n48-statement request; different input sets\nfrom slide 5. This does not explain the\nmodel's internal mechanism."
    },
    {
      "idx": 10,
      "label": "11",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Reward training scores the model's\nattempts and adjusts its learned behavior.\nWe tried correct-answer reward alone, then\na separate run adding agreement with\nPython's calculation from helper replies.\nNeither improved the test count.\n\nIF ASKED: Each attempted 576 practice\nsolutions. Together they took about four\nhours and 39 minutes. Two missing tests\nexplain 55–57.\n\nCAUTION: Final numbers only; calculation\nreview is unfinished."
    },
    {
      "idx": 11,
      "label": "12",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: First select users using their\nlocation questions. Then count their\nnumber questions, which are different\nrecords. The prompt gave both steps.\nTraining raised verified successes from\ntwo to 29 out of 72.\n\nIF ASKED: The combination was absent from\nour examples; the input sets were reused.\n\nCAUTION: Missing results allow 2–13 versus\n29–30. We checked calculations, not just\neasy-to-guess zeros. This is not inventing\na plan."
    },
    {
      "idx": 12,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: Imagine being told to answer row\nseven but label it row eight. That name\npoints to another text in the request.\nAccuracy was 39% with such misleading\nnames, versus 80% with unrelated names.\n\nIF ASKED: Order still told the model which\nstatement to judge. Software forced the\nanswer names.\n\nCAUTION: Deliberate stress test, not\nnormal use. We observed output behavior,\nnot the model's attention."
    },
    {
      "idx": 13,
      "label": "14",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "SAY: This adds the third comparison\nomitted from slide five: use matching row\nnumbers rather than arbitrary names. Both\nhelped the two language models judge\nstatements.\n\nIF ASKED: At 64 statements, Qwen scored\n44%, 85%, 83%; Mistral 35%, 58%, 52%.\nOrder: no names, row numbers, arbitrary\nnames.\n\nCAUTION: Three Mistral arbitrary-name\nresponses had invalid formatting. Do not\nconclude row numbers are inherently\nbetter."
